1
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:05,250
Antarctica in summer.

2
00:00:07,125 --> 00:00:10,500
After months of darkness,
the sun has finally reappeared.

3
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:16,625
Ships can once again travel southwards.

4
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:22,083
They dare to venture into icy territory
only during the light season,

5
00:00:23,583 --> 00:00:27,458
but winter's rigid, icy carapace
is not easy to penetrate.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,083
Even for icebreakers,
voyages into South Polar Sea are risky.

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,750
Gigantic icebergs and stormy seas
are added dangers.

8
00:00:46,833 --> 00:00:49,500
The coastal areas
around the subantarctic islands

9
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:50,875
are mostly ice-free,

10
00:00:52,166 --> 00:00:56,541
serving as the habitat
for Antarctic's remarkably abundant fauna.

11
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:08,958
These narrow strips of land
serve as a fighting arena,

12
00:01:09,083 --> 00:01:11,708
marriage market and breeding ground.

13
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:33,833
The Antarctic continent is enclosed
by a vast expanse of ice.

14
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:44,166
Emperor penguins have no problem
with the inhospitable conditions.

15
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:55,125
They're the largest penguin species.

16
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:57,416
The southernmost continent is their home,

17
00:01:57,916 --> 00:01:58,791
their kingdom.

18
00:02:01,083 --> 00:02:03,666
To us humans, it consists of tales,

19
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:07,541
tales of Antarctica,
from the End of the World.

20
00:02:37,375 --> 00:02:40,957
The gateway to Antarctica
lies in the far south of Argentina,

21
00:02:42,625 --> 00:02:43,957
Tierra del Fuego.

22
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:53,875
The sea is the best connection
to the rest of the world.

23
00:02:55,792 --> 00:02:59,917
One of the few places that exist here
is renowned beyond the country's borders.

24
00:03:03,542 --> 00:03:07,875
It's the resort town of Ushuaia,
also known as the End of the World.

25
00:03:10,082 --> 00:03:12,750
Tapering off into the foothills
of the Andes Mountains

26
00:03:12,917 --> 00:03:15,167
is the southern end
of the American continent.

27
00:03:16,625 --> 00:03:21,000
Around 60,000 residents rely primarily
on fishing or tourism for sustenance.

28
00:03:21,667 --> 00:03:23,167
The season begins in October

29
00:03:23,292 --> 00:03:26,125
when visitors are lured
by the Southern Hemisphere summer.

30
00:03:33,082 --> 00:03:34,250
Not far from the city,

31
00:03:34,375 --> 00:03:37,042
it's breeding time
for Magellanic penguins.

32
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:48,625
Males and females share hatching duties,
going off in turns to find food.

33
00:03:57,125 --> 00:04:00,582
Only occasionally can they indulge
in some time together.

34
00:04:10,332 --> 00:04:13,375
Magellanic penguins live on fish,
squid and krill,

35
00:04:13,707 --> 00:04:18,083
and there's a plentiful supply of these
in the cold waters of Tierra del Fuego.

36
00:04:20,625 --> 00:04:24,750
Ushuaia is famous not only due to
the many cruise liners that stop here,

37
00:04:24,958 --> 00:04:28,208
but particularly in its role
as the gateway to Antarctica.

38
00:04:37,291 --> 00:04:39,958
It's the starting point
for numerous expeditions.

39
00:04:47,666 --> 00:04:52,208
The Kapitan Khlebnikov is getting ready
for its next voyage into South Polar Sea.

40
00:04:52,750 --> 00:04:55,958
Provisions are loaded,
and the last passengers board the vessel.

41
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:06,041
The best time to set out is the onset
of the local summer in November.

42
00:05:12,166 --> 00:05:17,041
The lighthouse, Faro Les Éclaireurs, lies
around eight nautical miles from Ushuaia.

43
00:05:17,458 --> 00:05:20,875
It marks the end of the habitable world
for human beings.

44
00:05:24,041 --> 00:05:26,791
Animals, on the other hand,
are very much at home here,

45
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,500
as the waters are teeming
with sustenance for them.

46
00:05:36,375 --> 00:05:40,833
South American sea lions will often choose
small, rocky islets as their retreat.

47
00:05:41,458 --> 00:05:45,125
They rest and sleep here before embarking
on their next fishing foray.

48
00:05:52,416 --> 00:05:54,166
The males don't have much downtime,

49
00:05:54,250 --> 00:05:56,583
as they have to guard
and protect their harem.

50
00:06:03,583 --> 00:06:07,041
The South American blackish oystercatcher
feeds on mussels,

51
00:06:07,208 --> 00:06:09,208
which it cracks open with its hard beak.

52
00:06:27,333 --> 00:06:31,125
The voyage of discovery goes from
the southern tip of the American continent

53
00:06:31,333 --> 00:06:33,208
to the South Georgia Islands.

54
00:06:42,541 --> 00:06:46,541
The Kapitan Khlebnikov is an icebreaker
with special technical equipment,

55
00:06:46,833 --> 00:06:49,500
making it well-suited
for the passage to Antarctica.

56
00:06:50,208 --> 00:06:53,541
A special radar system
sounds out the ice situation.

57
00:07:00,208 --> 00:07:03,583
Its reinforced hull is designed
to break through sea ice

58
00:07:03,666 --> 00:07:05,041
up to three meters thick.

59
00:07:09,291 --> 00:07:12,166
Although the conditions
don't yet call for this capability,

60
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:14,166
the voyage is still not an easy one.

61
00:07:14,458 --> 00:07:18,916
The erratic and often stormy weather here
has taught experienced seafarers

62
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,625
to have a healthy respect for the sea
at between 40 and 60 degrees latitude.

63
00:07:24,333 --> 00:07:27,291
They've given it nicknames,
such as the Roaring 40s,

64
00:07:27,625 --> 00:07:31,708
the Furious 50s,
and the Shrieking or Screaming 60s.

65
00:07:39,375 --> 00:07:41,583
Gulls and other seabirds
accompany the ship,

66
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,125
defying the challenges of the wild sea.

67
00:07:55,583 --> 00:08:00,041
South Georgia lies some
2,000 kilometers east of Tierra del Fuego,

68
00:08:00,291 --> 00:08:02,125
part of the subantarctic islands.

69
00:08:06,208 --> 00:08:08,916
Apart from a British governor
and two scientists,

70
00:08:09,041 --> 00:08:10,500
the island is uninhabited.

71
00:08:14,625 --> 00:08:17,583
Mountains, snow and ice
dominate the landscape.

72
00:08:18,666 --> 00:08:23,250
At 2,934 meters,
Mount Paget is the highest peak.

73
00:08:25,208 --> 00:08:29,375
All around the island,
more than 160 glaciers flow into the sea,

74
00:08:31,166 --> 00:08:33,875
but the coast is ice-free
almost all year round.

75
00:08:41,458 --> 00:08:45,666
Robust bull kelp, up to ten meters long,
grows in abundance here.

76
00:08:48,666 --> 00:08:52,291
It's generally a safe environment
for fish, mussels and crabs,

77
00:08:57,583 --> 00:09:01,000
except when the southern giant petrel
is hunting for food.

78
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:16,791
King penguins are the most
imposing residents of South Georgia.

79
00:09:19,250 --> 00:09:22,750
They gather in the island's bays
in the Southern Hemisphere spring.

80
00:09:30,375 --> 00:09:34,083
The males arrive first and look
for their breeding place from last year.

81
00:09:47,166 --> 00:09:50,458
When they dive into the water,
they do it together in groups.

82
00:09:50,708 --> 00:09:54,583
It's a reliable survival strategy
to escape predators lurking underwater.

83
00:10:04,541 --> 00:10:07,708
King penguins are sociable
and like to hunt together.

84
00:10:08,208 --> 00:10:10,208
Their prey is mainly fish and squid.

85
00:10:14,333 --> 00:10:19,166
After about 150 daily dives,
they seek a resting place on the beach.

86
00:10:21,375 --> 00:10:24,625
The narrow, ice-free beaches
between the glaciers and the sea

87
00:10:24,791 --> 00:10:26,875
provide shelter from the wind and weather,

88
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,625
not only for penguins
but also for seals and other creatures.

89
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:37,750
A couple of weeks after the males,

90
00:10:37,875 --> 00:10:40,875
the females follow
and begin to seek out their partner.

91
00:10:46,208 --> 00:10:49,625
During the breeding season,
king penguins are strictly monogamous.

92
00:10:53,416 --> 00:10:57,125
On land, adult penguins don't have
any natural predators in South Georgia,

93
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:05,583
but not all of them will return
from their long fishing trips in the sea.

94
00:11:06,125 --> 00:11:08,291
Orcas and other predators take their toll.

95
00:11:14,291 --> 00:11:17,125
Some penguins wait in vain
for their partner to return.

96
00:11:28,625 --> 00:11:32,416
Their distinctive plumage shields them
from the cold and wetness.

97
00:11:32,666 --> 00:11:35,541
The feathers atop
the underlying warm downy layer

98
00:11:35,708 --> 00:11:37,791
must be preened and oiled regularly.

99
00:11:44,125 --> 00:11:46,875
King penguins aren't
the only residents here.

100
00:11:50,958 --> 00:11:51,833
In the summer,

101
00:11:51,916 --> 00:11:54,958
the beaches of South Georgia
are positively bustling.

102
00:12:00,750 --> 00:12:04,708
An elephant seal colony obstructs the path
from the breeding ground to the sea.

103
00:12:11,083 --> 00:12:14,416
Only the adult males
sport the prominent eponymous trunk.

104
00:12:15,041 --> 00:12:17,541
These giants can weigh up to four tons.

105
00:12:20,416 --> 00:12:23,583
Young males don't have much
to show in the nose department yet.

106
00:12:27,291 --> 00:12:31,041
It takes about eight years
for the olfactory organ to reach a size

107
00:12:31,125 --> 00:12:33,166
where the wearer can make an impression.

108
00:12:45,416 --> 00:12:47,625
They learn how to fight early on.

109
00:12:49,958 --> 00:12:52,416
The young elephant seals
don't hold back either.

110
00:13:08,250 --> 00:13:11,625
Strength, aggressiveness
and tactics are all decisive factors

111
00:13:11,708 --> 00:13:14,125
in the fight for females and territory.

112
00:13:14,625 --> 00:13:16,541
It's often a matter of life and death.

113
00:13:20,291 --> 00:13:21,541
They have to start young.

114
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:23,250
Practice makes perfect.

115
00:13:32,708 --> 00:13:36,125
His huge trunk and fighting ability
have earned him respect,

116
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:37,916
and he's got what he wanted.

117
00:13:45,625 --> 00:13:48,250
The bull has gathered
15 females around him,

118
00:13:48,458 --> 00:13:50,125
a respectable-sized harem.

119
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:53,416
They are mostly peaceable
and take care of their offspring.

120
00:13:57,291 --> 00:14:00,916
This young fellow can still
only dream of future conquests.

121
00:14:04,958 --> 00:14:07,666
It's good to relax,
surrounded by the family.

122
00:14:11,333 --> 00:14:13,125
Wailing babies are part of it too.

123
00:14:15,625 --> 00:14:18,916
The baby seal needs plenty of milk
and has to grow quickly.

124
00:14:21,125 --> 00:14:22,958
There isn't much time for having fun.

125
00:14:23,208 --> 00:14:27,208
Elephant seal pups must become
independent in just four weeks.

126
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:35,333
Life still seems carefree,
but in just a few weeks,

127
00:14:35,416 --> 00:14:38,208
it will be time to start out
on their long ocean journey.

128
00:14:44,958 --> 00:14:49,958
Till then, the seal pups quadruple
their birth weight to about 150 kilograms.

129
00:15:00,541 --> 00:15:03,083
It's vital for them
to have a thick layer of fat

130
00:15:03,291 --> 00:15:05,958
so they can survive
in the icy South Polar Sea.

131
00:15:14,541 --> 00:15:17,583
Elephant seals only go ashore
during the mating season

132
00:15:17,833 --> 00:15:20,250
to rear their young
and for their annual molt.

133
00:15:22,958 --> 00:15:26,375
The remaining time is dedicated
to hunting fish and squid in the sea.

134
00:15:27,166 --> 00:15:28,666
They spend months in the ocean

135
00:15:28,750 --> 00:15:32,166
until their reproductive instinct
drives them to go ashore again.

136
00:15:36,625 --> 00:15:38,250
South Georgia is a meeting point

137
00:15:38,333 --> 00:15:41,291
for many animals who otherwise
spend their lives in the sea.

138
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:46,875
The craggy interior of the island
is dominated by glaciers, ice and snow,

139
00:15:47,333 --> 00:15:49,583
not a good habitat for flora and fauna.

140
00:15:53,791 --> 00:15:55,583
What sparse vegetation there is

141
00:15:55,833 --> 00:15:58,375
can mostly be found
along the narrow coastal strip.

142
00:16:01,333 --> 00:16:05,250
However, mosses thrive
in the subantarctic's damp, cold climate,

143
00:16:09,958 --> 00:16:13,708
although temperatures in summer
rarely climb above ten degrees centigrade.

144
00:16:17,333 --> 00:16:20,375
Countless small streams are fed
by rain and glaciers.

145
00:16:20,791 --> 00:16:23,041
There's no shortage of water
in South Georgia.

146
00:16:31,416 --> 00:16:33,333
King penguins seem to like it here.

147
00:16:35,083 --> 00:16:37,958
After all, there are about 40,000 of them.

148
00:16:43,375 --> 00:16:47,333
They have an unusually delayed
breeding cycle of around 14 months.

149
00:16:47,791 --> 00:16:50,833
The result is that
a colony will contain year-old chicks,

150
00:16:51,041 --> 00:16:53,916
as well as adults
who are still searching for a partner.

151
00:16:58,708 --> 00:17:01,666
In winter, the young penguins
stay alone on land

152
00:17:01,875 --> 00:17:04,916
and wait for their parents
to return after months with food.

153
00:17:10,666 --> 00:17:13,625
It's summer,
the time for them to be raised and fed.

154
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:21,416
After about 14 months,

155
00:17:21,541 --> 00:17:24,708
they exchange their fluffy down
for waterproof plumage.

156
00:17:25,250 --> 00:17:28,375
Only then are they ready
to go fishing in the sea on their own.

157
00:17:35,833 --> 00:17:38,041
While one family is still
raising its young,

158
00:17:38,250 --> 00:17:41,333
an adult singleton is
on the lookout for a new partner.

159
00:17:55,333 --> 00:17:58,291
Once they find each other,
the courtship begins.

160
00:18:02,583 --> 00:18:05,208
It's a ritual that always leads
to the same outcome:

161
00:18:09,416 --> 00:18:10,291
mating.

162
00:18:23,458 --> 00:18:26,541
Not a simple business,
but definitely well worth it.

163
00:18:31,208 --> 00:18:34,458
The Antarctic fur seal nearby
poses no danger.

164
00:18:34,666 --> 00:18:36,750
He just has to keep an eye on his females.

165
00:18:40,333 --> 00:18:42,666
Fur seals are unperturbed
by the bad weather.

166
00:18:42,875 --> 00:18:44,875
They spend most of their time in water.

167
00:18:49,333 --> 00:18:51,291
On land, it's all about reproduction.

168
00:18:51,833 --> 00:18:54,666
There's often bitter fighting
over females and territory.

169
00:18:59,250 --> 00:19:01,833
Penguins and fur seals
rarely bother each other.

170
00:19:02,250 --> 00:19:04,791
Encounters are marked mainly by curiosity

171
00:19:10,083 --> 00:19:11,125
or mistrust.

172
00:19:19,666 --> 00:19:22,250
After careful inspection
and risk assessment,

173
00:19:22,541 --> 00:19:23,875
they each go on their way.

174
00:19:30,916 --> 00:19:33,708
King penguins often breed
near gentoo penguins.

175
00:19:37,541 --> 00:19:39,375
Their trumpeting call is distinctive

176
00:19:39,458 --> 00:19:42,458
and led to their being known
as donkey penguins in German.

177
00:19:44,541 --> 00:19:47,666
They also use the short Austral summer
to rear their young.

178
00:19:56,166 --> 00:19:58,791
Their nests are built of stones
and grassy padding.

179
00:19:59,416 --> 00:20:01,916
They also don't mind
stealing from their neighbors.

180
00:20:08,583 --> 00:20:12,500
The eggs are incubated
for around 30 days until the chicks hatch.

181
00:20:17,291 --> 00:20:21,208
After that, the young typically stay
in the nest for another 30 days,

182
00:20:21,458 --> 00:20:23,708
kept warm and protected by their parents.

183
00:20:33,458 --> 00:20:36,916
The parent birds will have to find
over 100 kilograms of krill

184
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:38,708
before their chick is fully grown.

185
00:20:43,333 --> 00:20:45,041
They take turns to forage.

186
00:20:45,416 --> 00:20:48,583
One parent is always left behind
to protect their young.

187
00:20:52,458 --> 00:20:56,291
Gentoo penguins only breed
in regions without snow or ice.

188
00:20:56,708 --> 00:21:00,125
In South Georgia, every year,
they revisit their old breeding sites.

189
00:21:16,541 --> 00:21:18,250
Good neighborliness is important,

190
00:21:18,375 --> 00:21:21,500
as there's limited ice-free space
available on the island.

191
00:21:23,916 --> 00:21:25,666
Everyone claims their own place.

192
00:21:28,625 --> 00:21:33,416
The fur seal pups are born late November,
at the start of the Austral summer.

193
00:21:36,166 --> 00:21:40,833
The baby seals often stay alone for days
while their mothers are out hunting food.

194
00:21:43,291 --> 00:21:46,083
Mothers come ashore
only briefly to nurse their pups.

195
00:21:50,333 --> 00:21:52,916
Fur seal pups are used
to being left on their own.

196
00:22:00,125 --> 00:22:01,750
They huddle together for comfort.

197
00:22:09,416 --> 00:22:12,500
Now and then,
a quarrel breaks out between adult seals,

198
00:22:12,875 --> 00:22:17,000
but the worst of the fights are over,
with territories already staked out.

199
00:22:20,125 --> 00:22:24,291
All in all, the cohabitation
of all these different species works well.

200
00:22:27,208 --> 00:22:32,333
Fur seals, elephant seals, or penguins,
in South Georgia, there's room for all.

201
00:22:37,333 --> 00:22:40,291
The reason why so many animals
are drawn to South Georgia

202
00:22:40,458 --> 00:22:42,250
is its bountiful hunting grounds.

203
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:53,333
The sea here has
a wealth of nutrition resources,

204
00:22:53,625 --> 00:22:56,583
making the archipelago
one of the most popular ports of call

205
00:22:56,666 --> 00:22:57,958
in the South Polar Sea.

206
00:23:02,333 --> 00:23:06,666
South Georgia lies at the conjunction
of the Atlantic Ocean and Antarctica.

207
00:23:07,541 --> 00:23:10,541
Heading south,
the route passes by the Sandwich Islands

208
00:23:10,625 --> 00:23:15,125
to the Antarctic Peninsula,
traveling ever farther into the polar sea.

209
00:23:18,791 --> 00:23:20,666
The weather here is very changeable.

210
00:23:23,291 --> 00:23:26,833
Cold winds from the south meet
warm air masses from the north.

211
00:23:28,208 --> 00:23:30,791
This results in tremendous
low-pressure areas.

212
00:23:31,208 --> 00:23:34,083
Storms and bad weather
are a foregone conclusion.

213
00:23:35,458 --> 00:23:37,250
It's challenging for any expedition.

214
00:23:46,291 --> 00:23:47,791
The first icebergs appear,

215
00:23:48,125 --> 00:23:51,958
gigantic behemoths that have broken off
from the Antarctic ice sheet,

216
00:23:52,125 --> 00:23:53,291
and are drifting north.

217
00:23:55,291 --> 00:23:59,875
The sea gnaws away at them incessantly,
but the water is only a few degrees warm,

218
00:24:00,041 --> 00:24:01,916
so it takes years for them to melt.

219
00:24:12,416 --> 00:24:15,083
Icebergs often travel
thousands of kilometers,

220
00:24:15,250 --> 00:24:17,375
driven by tides and stormy winds.

221
00:24:26,458 --> 00:24:30,583
Only one-seventh of their mass sticks out
above the surface of the water.

222
00:24:31,791 --> 00:24:34,541
The main section underwater
is not only much deeper,

223
00:24:34,708 --> 00:24:37,833
it's also much wider,
very dangerous for shipping.

224
00:24:50,458 --> 00:24:54,000
Near the Southern Antarctic Circle,
the sun hardly sets at all.

225
00:24:56,458 --> 00:24:58,583
It's good for expeditions
to the Antarctic.

226
00:25:12,583 --> 00:25:14,791
Volcanic islands jut out of the sea,

227
00:25:15,041 --> 00:25:18,416
evidence of the massive
geological activity under the seabed,

228
00:25:18,666 --> 00:25:22,625
where the oceanic plate is sliding
under the Antarctic continental plate.

229
00:25:28,375 --> 00:25:33,041
The greater part of the Southern Continent
is constantly covered by a huge ice sheet.

230
00:25:39,416 --> 00:25:43,083
In summer,
only 2% of the continent is free of ice.

231
00:25:43,625 --> 00:25:46,958
Most of this ice-free area
is in the Antarctic Peninsula.

232
00:25:49,458 --> 00:25:54,000
The nutrient-rich open water attracts
birds and other marine life to the area.

233
00:26:00,541 --> 00:26:03,875
It's just one of many stops
for the Kapitan Khlebnikov.

234
00:26:14,750 --> 00:26:18,083
The expeditionary ship moors
not far from the coastline.

235
00:26:30,625 --> 00:26:34,041
Frequent low-pressure systems
make the local climate milder

236
00:26:34,125 --> 00:26:35,833
than on the rest of the continent.

237
00:26:37,083 --> 00:26:41,208
Lone gentoo penguins find ice-free spots
to go about their business.

238
00:26:46,083 --> 00:26:47,875
They're usually not that sociable,

239
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,708
preferring close contact
only with their partner.

240
00:27:04,416 --> 00:27:08,291
If both of them like the look of a place,
that will be their nesting site.

241
00:27:12,500 --> 00:27:15,041
They only form colonies
during the breeding season.

242
00:27:15,416 --> 00:27:19,125
This protects the chicks better
against attacks from predatory seabirds.

243
00:27:22,583 --> 00:27:25,791
The lack of vegetation restricts
the choice of building materials

244
00:27:25,875 --> 00:27:26,750
for their nests.

245
00:27:27,416 --> 00:27:28,916
There are only small stones.

246
00:27:32,458 --> 00:27:34,125
These two are just starting out,

247
00:27:37,125 --> 00:27:39,708
but they have managed
to agree on a breeding spot.

248
00:27:49,666 --> 00:27:52,166
Mating is just taking a little longer,

249
00:27:57,041 --> 00:27:59,458
but true love will always find a way.

250
00:28:10,208 --> 00:28:12,458
Others are already incubating their eggs,

251
00:28:18,250 --> 00:28:22,791
while their partners waddle off
to the sea to stock up on food reserves.

252
00:28:25,416 --> 00:28:29,416
On their way to the water,
they meet some excitable Adélie penguins.

253
00:28:32,166 --> 00:28:35,875
Adélie penguins are the most common
penguin species in Antarctica

254
00:28:36,083 --> 00:28:38,166
and can be found throughout the continent.

255
00:28:47,208 --> 00:28:49,833
They're remarkably agile walkers on land.

256
00:28:50,166 --> 00:28:54,500
A speed of about 2.5 kilometers per hour
is great pace for their small webbed feet.

257
00:28:58,416 --> 00:29:02,333
They often walk long distances to reach
their hunting grounds in the open sea.

258
00:29:03,208 --> 00:29:06,291
Distances of about 50 kilometers
are not uncommon.

259
00:29:16,250 --> 00:29:18,625
To make their dangerous dive
into the water,

260
00:29:18,875 --> 00:29:21,458
they usually gather at places
where the sea is rough.

261
00:29:25,375 --> 00:29:29,541
Diving into the murky water together
makes it more difficult for leopard seals

262
00:29:29,625 --> 00:29:32,625
that might be lurking,
waiting to grab one of them.

263
00:29:45,041 --> 00:29:48,458
Like all penguins,
they appear to be quite clumsy on land.

264
00:29:48,750 --> 00:29:52,083
Their streamlined bodies are
best suited to a life in the water.

265
00:29:57,750 --> 00:29:59,333
They feed mostly on krill,

266
00:29:59,583 --> 00:30:02,500
tiny crustaceans
that are abundant in the polar sea.

267
00:30:05,125 --> 00:30:07,708
This is why they breed
in the millions here,

268
00:30:07,791 --> 00:30:09,708
on the icy Southern Continent.

269
00:30:23,375 --> 00:30:26,208
The Antarctic Peninsula
is still the warmest place.

270
00:30:26,541 --> 00:30:29,166
In summer, temperatures can reach
above freezing,

271
00:30:29,541 --> 00:30:32,458
melting the ice
and creating bizarre formations.

272
00:30:36,041 --> 00:30:40,416
Sometimes a gigantic table iceberg
gets stranded near the coast.

273
00:30:54,125 --> 00:30:55,625
It can be stuck for years

274
00:30:55,750 --> 00:30:58,750
until it melts enough
to be able to continue its odyssey.

275
00:31:21,333 --> 00:31:24,458
Only icebreakers dare
advance through the pack ice.

276
00:31:27,375 --> 00:31:28,291
So for most ships,

277
00:31:28,375 --> 00:31:31,375
the Antarctic Peninsula
is their last port of call.

278
00:31:40,541 --> 00:31:43,333
The extreme icy conditions
make it impossible

279
00:31:43,416 --> 00:31:46,416
to continue their journey
into the south polar region

280
00:31:46,708 --> 00:31:48,083
or through the Weddell Sea.

281
00:31:55,666 --> 00:31:56,958
Even in the summer months,

282
00:31:57,041 --> 00:32:00,333
the ice prevents
any headway being made in many places.

283
00:32:19,208 --> 00:32:23,875
Occasionally, the Kapitan Khlebnikov
will plow through less obstructed areas.

284
00:32:24,625 --> 00:32:26,916
With the ice freezing
and melting all the time,

285
00:32:27,083 --> 00:32:29,000
conditions change constantly.

286
00:32:32,125 --> 00:32:35,625
Seawater freezes
at minus 1.8 degrees centigrade.

287
00:32:35,958 --> 00:32:40,875
It forms a pulpy mass of ice crystals
that join together to create ice floes.

288
00:32:41,750 --> 00:32:45,875
In rough seas, these floes collide
with each other and form thick plates.

289
00:32:46,375 --> 00:32:48,041
This is called pancake ice.

290
00:32:52,041 --> 00:32:55,166
In winter, these floes become
deeper and wider,

291
00:32:55,291 --> 00:32:58,833
finally forming
a 1,000-kilometer-wide ice shield

292
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:00,416
around the whole continent.

293
00:33:05,250 --> 00:33:08,125
It is only in spring,
when the ice begins to crack,

294
00:33:08,291 --> 00:33:11,583
that ice-breakers can penetrate
once again as far as this.

295
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:17,875
Until then,

296
00:33:18,083 --> 00:33:22,875
around 20 million square kilometers
of solid ice separates the continent

297
00:33:22,958 --> 00:33:24,333
from the rest of the world.

298
00:33:41,500 --> 00:33:45,375
The Kapitan Khlebnikov can deal with
pack ice several meters thick,

299
00:33:48,500 --> 00:33:51,458
but traveling through
extensive sheets of ice is risky.

300
00:34:18,250 --> 00:34:21,791
A special radar system helps
to assess the ice conditions.

301
00:34:24,375 --> 00:34:27,041
The motto for all shipping
in the polar sea is:

302
00:34:27,333 --> 00:34:30,375
the shortest way through the ice
is around the ice.

303
00:34:35,125 --> 00:34:38,625
It's because a detour is still safer
than sailing straight through it.

304
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:42,708
However, this isn't always possible
in the Weddell Sea.

305
00:34:45,208 --> 00:34:49,541
When the radar can no longer assess
the ice floes and open water in between,

306
00:34:49,833 --> 00:34:51,625
they have to resort to a helicopter.

307
00:34:56,750 --> 00:35:00,958
On reconnaissance flights, they map out
the best path through the icy labyrinth.

308
00:35:15,291 --> 00:35:18,625
Even for a robust icebreaker
like the Kapitan Khlebnikov,

309
00:35:18,750 --> 00:35:21,750
navigating in the Antarctic Ocean
is not an easy business.

310
00:35:24,541 --> 00:35:28,541
Especially shaped bow
and massively reinforced steel cladding

311
00:35:28,625 --> 00:35:31,208
protect the hull
from the sharp edges of the ice.

312
00:35:34,458 --> 00:35:38,375
Powerful engines ensure that
the ship has enough propulsion and thrust.

313
00:36:10,333 --> 00:36:15,208
At last, the Kapitan Khlebnikov reaches
open water again and can make headway.

314
00:36:19,250 --> 00:36:20,625
The days are getting longer,

315
00:36:20,708 --> 00:36:23,500
which is helpful
when plotting a course through pack ice.

316
00:36:31,458 --> 00:36:33,000
The sun is low in the sky,

317
00:36:33,083 --> 00:36:35,666
but this far south,
it rarely sets completely.

318
00:36:46,750 --> 00:36:49,875
The Weddell Sea is famously known
as the iceberg factory.

319
00:36:50,416 --> 00:36:54,166
Gigantic chunks keep breaking off
of the ice shelf and drifting north.

320
00:37:03,541 --> 00:37:05,291
Along the Antarctic coastline,

321
00:37:05,458 --> 00:37:07,833
there are no natural harbors
to weigh anchor.

322
00:37:17,458 --> 00:37:19,958
The only option is to park in the ice.

323
00:37:34,833 --> 00:37:37,708
The ship plows through the ice
at maximum speed

324
00:37:44,333 --> 00:37:46,125
until it is brought to a halt

325
00:37:47,625 --> 00:37:50,500
in the midst of a vast,
blindingly white ice desert

326
00:37:50,625 --> 00:37:53,291
extending 2,000 kilometers to the south.

327
00:38:12,541 --> 00:38:15,250
There's no environment on Earth
more hostile than this.

328
00:38:15,583 --> 00:38:17,875
It would seem
as if nothing could survive here.

329
00:38:42,125 --> 00:38:44,416
The Kapitan Khlebnikov can take a rest.

330
00:38:44,875 --> 00:38:46,333
Frozen fast in the ice,

331
00:38:46,416 --> 00:38:49,541
it merely waits until the researchers
and expedition members

332
00:38:49,708 --> 00:38:51,291
are ready for the return voyage.

333
00:39:10,500 --> 00:39:15,291
Despite the adverse conditions,
this place is home to the emperor penguin.

334
00:39:21,583 --> 00:39:24,500
Its hunting grounds are accessed
through a hole in the ice.

335
00:39:30,291 --> 00:39:34,208
These penguins have caught their fill
and are now heading back to their colony.

336
00:39:40,791 --> 00:39:43,291
This could be several kilometers
from the water,

337
00:39:43,500 --> 00:39:45,791
a long trek for short penguin legs,

338
00:39:49,333 --> 00:39:52,750
but they have more than one method
of locomotion at their disposal.

339
00:39:58,791 --> 00:40:01,833
Their smooth, oiled feathers
make it easy for them

340
00:40:01,916 --> 00:40:04,541
to slide across the icy surface
beneath them.

341
00:40:14,666 --> 00:40:18,208
Emperor penguin colonies are often
far away from the sea,

342
00:40:18,458 --> 00:40:20,583
safe from the melting ice in spring.

343
00:40:25,250 --> 00:40:27,625
The icebergs they pass are immobile,

344
00:40:35,250 --> 00:40:38,583
frozen prisoners
of the white Antarctic wasteland.

345
00:40:44,791 --> 00:40:48,666
They stay here
until summer breaks up the sheet ice,

346
00:40:48,916 --> 00:40:50,666
freeing them from their captivity.

347
00:41:01,833 --> 00:41:04,666
Wind and weather create
bizarre formations,

348
00:41:10,041 --> 00:41:14,125
icy sculptures that almost look like
they were made by human hands.

349
00:41:21,583 --> 00:41:24,041
It's hard to believe
that this barren landscape

350
00:41:24,166 --> 00:41:25,750
could harbor any life at all,

351
00:41:26,833 --> 00:41:30,000
but it's one of the biggest
nurseries in the world.

352
00:41:41,375 --> 00:41:44,250
This is where emperor penguins
raise their young.

353
00:41:46,166 --> 00:41:47,708
It's a risky breeding strategy,

354
00:41:52,083 --> 00:41:53,916
but one that generally pays off.

355
00:41:58,083 --> 00:42:00,875
In such a desolate area,
there are few enemies,

356
00:42:01,083 --> 00:42:03,416
and the young can grow up
in relative safety.

357
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:11,916
Like any other chicks,
they are permanently hungry,

358
00:42:14,583 --> 00:42:17,125
so their parents
must bring back loads of food.

359
00:42:18,250 --> 00:42:20,625
The Antarctic summer
is the best time for this.

360
00:42:20,916 --> 00:42:23,708
The days are very long,
and the more the pack ice melts,

361
00:42:23,958 --> 00:42:26,291
the shorter their way
to open water becomes.

362
00:42:30,208 --> 00:42:34,458
They tirelessly tramp back and forth
between their colony and the sea.

363
00:42:37,458 --> 00:42:40,625
The cold sea is teeming
with fish, squid and krill,

364
00:42:40,958 --> 00:42:44,916
so the adult penguins can always bring
plenty of food back to their young.

365
00:42:47,083 --> 00:42:49,583
There are more than
40 colonies in the Antarctic,

366
00:42:49,833 --> 00:42:53,583
and year after year,
emperor penguins return to the same ones.

367
00:42:55,250 --> 00:42:58,000
In the deepest winter,
they breed by the thousands

368
00:42:58,166 --> 00:43:00,666
and use the short summers
to raise their offspring.

369
00:43:03,458 --> 00:43:06,666
Now there's food in abundance,
and the chicks grow fast.

370
00:43:09,333 --> 00:43:13,041
There's plenty of pre-digested fish daily.

371
00:43:30,541 --> 00:43:34,916
The fluffy down plumage gives the chicks
excellent protection against the cold.

372
00:43:45,500 --> 00:43:49,208
The little ones are insatiable,
but begging doesn't always work.

373
00:43:53,125 --> 00:43:55,958
When the supplies
in the parent's stomach have been eaten,

374
00:43:56,166 --> 00:43:58,333
they have to set off again
to get more food.

375
00:44:06,666 --> 00:44:08,000
The chicks often sleep

376
00:44:08,083 --> 00:44:10,333
while their parents are away
for long periods,

377
00:44:10,583 --> 00:44:11,875
waiting for their return.

378
00:44:19,583 --> 00:44:24,125
Emperor penguins are the only animals
that reside primarily in the Antarctic.

379
00:44:33,500 --> 00:44:35,250
Their bodies are perfectly adapted

380
00:44:35,333 --> 00:44:37,875
to the adverse conditions
of the icy continent.

381
00:44:41,500 --> 00:44:44,625
Their plumage protects them from the cold.

382
00:44:45,208 --> 00:44:47,416
A base layer of warm down is covered

383
00:44:47,500 --> 00:44:50,250
by a closely growing layer
of short feathers.

384
00:44:55,208 --> 00:44:59,583
The top feathers are constantly oiled
by a secretion from the preen gland,

385
00:44:59,833 --> 00:45:02,583
making the penguin's body
completely waterproof.

386
00:45:05,125 --> 00:45:07,000
They also have a thick layer of fat,

387
00:45:07,166 --> 00:45:11,458
which helps them to withstand temperatures
of up to minus 60 degrees centigrade.

388
00:45:14,875 --> 00:45:18,625
Loud calls help them
to identify partners and offspring.

389
00:45:24,541 --> 00:45:27,416
Emperor penguins are the largest
of the penguin species.

390
00:45:27,750 --> 00:45:32,541
They can reach up to 1.3 meters in height
and weigh around 38 kilograms.

391
00:45:34,041 --> 00:45:37,083
In winter, they can lose
up to 40% of their body weight.

392
00:45:37,666 --> 00:45:40,458
Now, during the summer months,
they stock up again.

393
00:45:43,625 --> 00:45:49,500
When both parents are away foraging,
the young penguins gather in nurseries.

394
00:45:51,583 --> 00:45:54,750
They're constantly supervised
and guarded by adult birds.

395
00:45:56,208 --> 00:46:00,541
Occasionally, danger can threaten
in the form of seagulls and giant petrels.

396
00:46:01,000 --> 00:46:02,833
They're safer when they're in groups.

397
00:46:04,583 --> 00:46:07,750
A lost chick might not be able
to find its parents again.

398
00:46:08,166 --> 00:46:10,333
If that happens,
then the chick will starve.

399
00:46:26,291 --> 00:46:29,166
In the safety of the group
and looked after by adults,

400
00:46:29,333 --> 00:46:31,291
the chicks often go on outings.

401
00:46:38,500 --> 00:46:42,500
Here and there, the walking party
is joined by others keen on a stroll.

402
00:47:00,333 --> 00:47:04,541
In a few weeks, they'll grow
their proper waterproof penguin plumage.

403
00:47:04,916 --> 00:47:05,833
When that happens,

404
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:10,000
they'll be able to go down to the water
and begin their lives as young adults.

405
00:47:40,541 --> 00:47:43,208
The chicks receive constant care
from both parents.

406
00:47:43,541 --> 00:47:45,791
It's almost impossible for a single parent

407
00:47:45,916 --> 00:47:48,916
to catch enough food
to raise a young chick on its own.

408
00:47:58,500 --> 00:48:00,875
When the temperature reaches
minus 20 degrees,

409
00:48:00,958 --> 00:48:02,875
it gets too warm for the penguins.

410
00:48:03,500 --> 00:48:05,583
This results in colonies spreading out.

411
00:48:14,458 --> 00:48:18,083
The families redistribute themselves
and form scattered groups.

412
00:48:32,458 --> 00:48:37,000
The fishing forays that would take months
in the winter, now take just a day or so.

413
00:48:43,916 --> 00:48:47,583
Toward evening,
most penguins return to their colony.

414
00:48:52,916 --> 00:48:55,916
They will spend
the short Antarctic summer nights there,

415
00:48:56,291 --> 00:48:58,041
in the safety of their own family.

416
00:49:12,333 --> 00:49:16,500
For emperor penguins,
this is their home and retreat.

417
00:49:21,166 --> 00:49:24,958
As humans, we wonder
about the marvels of this world.

418
00:49:31,083 --> 00:49:35,375
Only a few of us ever have access to it
and return to tell its tales,

419
00:49:38,583 --> 00:49:42,125
tales of life and death,
friendship and family

420
00:49:42,625 --> 00:49:44,375
amid the harshest conditions,

421
00:49:51,125 --> 00:49:54,458
tales of Antarctica,
from the End of the World.

